 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "dyson keeps pulsin on n off like its stutterin, wont stay on proper. batt is full. pls help"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "I can help with that. Pulsing on and off is usually the vacuum's protection system kicking in because it detects a blockage or a wet filter. It helps to know exactly which parts to check.\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — A vacuum that pulses or cuts out after running is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage starving the airflow — check and clear those before suspecting battery, charger or motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its a v8 animal. no burning smell or heat or anything, battery looks fine. what do i check first?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Switch it off and unplug it (or take the battery off) first. Since the battery is charged and there's no heat, the pulsing is almost certainly the vacuum's safety system cutting power because it detects restricted airflow. This happens if the filter is damp or clogged, or if something is blocking the tube. Please do this next:\n1. Remove the filters (there are usually two: a pre-motor filter and a post-motor HEPA filter). Wash them in cold water only—never use detergent—and let them dry completely for 24 hours. Even slight dampness causes pulsing.\n2. With the battery out, detach the wand and floorhead and shine a light through each part to check for blockages (coins, hair, etc.). Once you've done that, let me know if you found any blockages or if the filters were wet/dirty."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok did the filter, its grey not purple lol. washed it, water came out black, left it overnight on radiator side. pulses way less but still a bit"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "The fact that it pulses less is a good sign—it suggests the airflow restriction was indeed the filter. However, Dyson filters are thick; drying them on a radiator often leaves the inner core damp while the outside feels dry, which still triggers the safety cut-out. Please detach the wand and floorhead and shine a torch through them to ensure there are no blockages (hair, coins) restricting airflow. Also, remove the battery and wipe the metal contacts on both the battery and the vacuum body with a dry cloth to rule out a poor connection."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "checked the wand, big clump of hair and cat litter stuck at the bottom where the head goes on, pulled it out. its fine now, proper suction, no pulsin"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That’s excellent news. The pulsing was the vacuum's safety system cutting power because it couldn't get enough air through that blockage of hair and cat litter. Just a quick tip for next time: Dyson filters take a full 24 hours to dry completely even if they feel dry on the outside, so try to avoid drying them directly on a radiator as that can trap moisture inside. If this happens again, check the wand and floorhead inlet first before worrying about the filter. Is there anything else I can help you with?"
  }
 ],
